5:["$","article",null,{"className":"container-site py-14 md:py-20","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"group inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-left h-4 w-4 transition-transform duration-300 group-hover:-translate-x-0.5 motion-reduce:transform-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","1l729n",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7"}],["$","path","x3x0zl",{"d":"M19 12H5"}],"$undefined"]}],"All writing"]}],["$","header",null,{"className":"mt-8 max-w-2xl","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[12px] tracking-wide text-muted-foreground/70","children":[["$","time",null,{"dateTime":"2026-09-27","children":"September 27, 2026"}],["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"h-px w-6 bg-border"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-clock h-3 w-3","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","mmk7yg",{"d":"M12 6v6l4 2"}],["$","circle","1mglay",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"10"}],"$undefined"]}],5," min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl","children":"Learning web security by building inspection tools"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Reading about the OWASP Top 10 is a map, not a journey. How building verification and inspection tools — scoped honestly — is teaching me web security from the evidence outward."}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-2","aria-label":"Tags","children":[["$","li","Cybersecurity",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Cybersecurity"}],["$","li","Web",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Web"}],["$","li","Learning",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Learning"}]]}]]}],["$","$Lf",null,{"className":"mt-12","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-2xl space-y-5","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Security is the field where reading the most and understanding the least is easiest. I can recite the OWASP Top 10, name the vulnerability classes, and still not be able to look at a live website and tell you anything true about its exposure. That gap — between vocabulary and perception — is the actual problem a security student has to solve."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"My answer has been to build tools that force me to look. Two projects carry most of that weight: Verify Hub, which checks whether websites are up, rendering correctly and configured sanely; and a web-intelligence concept that examines what sites expose about themselves. Neither is a security scanner. Both are instruments for learning to see."}],["$","h2","2",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"The map is not the territory"}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Reading about SQL injection is a map. Noticing that an error page leaks a stack trace with a framework version, forming a hypothesis about what that enables, and checking it carefully — that is the territory. Building inspection tools moved me from the first activity to the second, because a tool has to answer real questions with real evidence or it produces nothing at all."}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13","$L14","$L15","$L16","$L17"]}]}],"$L18"]}]
10:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"So the web-intelligence concept is built around evidence chains: every finding carries the observation behind it and a confidence framing. Nothing is reported that cannot show its source. That constraint is partly honesty and partly pedagogy — when your tool refuses to overstate, you inherit the habit."}]
11:["$","blockquote","5",{"className":"rounded-lg border-l-2 border-accent-blue bg-muted/30 px-6 py-5","children":["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[15px] italic leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Every finding is a lead, not a verdict. The moment your tool claims certainty it doesn't have, you have taught yourself to stop thinking."}]}]
12:["$","h2","6",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"Scoping is a security skill"}]
13:["$","p","7",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"The most important security engineering I have done is deciding what my tools will not do. Verify Hub checks availability, rendering and configuration — and its documentation says so, plainly, because a verification tool that implies it is a comprehensive security scanner is itself a security problem. It would produce false confidence, which is a vulnerability in the operator, not the target."}]
14:["$","ul","8",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Test only what you are authorised to test — authorisation is not a formality, it is the boundary of the work."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Report what was checked, not what was implied — scope belongs in the report, not the README alone."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Flag potential issues as leads for human review — 'potential' must mean 'unconfirmed' or it means nothing."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Prefer observability to exploitation — seeing the mechanism is the skill; damaging nothing is the discipline."]}]]}]
15:["$","h2","9",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"What I would tell another student"}]
16:["$","p","10",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Build the small, honest version of the tool you wish existed. A link checker that reports a screenshot and three facts about a page's configuration, built properly, will teach you more than a grand plan for a scanner you will never finish. You will meet HTTP headers as things with consequences, browser automation as an evidence-collection discipline, and error pages as objects of genuine interest."}]
17:["$","p","11",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"And hold the line on language. Security is a field where overstatement is easy and consequences are real. Say what you checked. Say what you found. Leave the certainty to the evidence. That discipline — more than any tool or technique — is the foundation the rest of security gets built on."}]
